5707 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055708 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055708 #edb/ref 1061 453948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055708 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8eef6615-9b8a-526e-9e9e-25070edfdad2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055708 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055709 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055709 #edb/ref 1061 453949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055709 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9d51bf5e-1d13-5f8d-9fd6-c02b852842c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055709 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055710 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055710 #edb/ref 1061 453952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055710 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a21ae939-d688-510c-9d02-aec18c105046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055710 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055711 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055711 #edb/ref 1061 453955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055711 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "68735c4a-f7f9-5265-80af-ded103195f00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055711 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055712 #edb/ref 1061 453956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055712 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8e354a30-502e-5775-810b-ba93046cedfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055712 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055959 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ in the first quadrant, measured in the usual way.\n\n![](images/bc/bca60509a15e4d32d06d96c7cf5eac65a00b4e5cb72242152dd998b933209d7d.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the $x$-coordinate of $P$ coincides with the cosine of the angle $\\theta,$ and\n\n- the $y$-coordinate coincides with the sine of the angle $\\theta.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nConsequently, if we know the angle $\\theta,$ we can easily work out the coordinates of the point $P,$ and vice-versa. This works regardless of whether the angle $\\theta$ is given in degrees or radians.\n\nFor now, we'll focus on points located in the first quadrant. Later, we will encounter points in all quadrants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055959 #edb/ref 1065 2493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055959 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8e9912a6-a3c6-5076-9724-a53f5e215dd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055959 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055960 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the unit circle and a point $P(x,y)$ that lies on the circle.\n\n![](images/3d/3d51ca417ba4f6f62bd3e5db5997842d0671f18920a7de70578146a49de79ec3.png)\n\nTo prove that $x=\\cos\\theta$ and $y=\\sin\\theta,$ let's examine the triangle that's formed by $\\overline{OP}$ and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/53/53d84bedcddcb484bc613a2f01e002afe16b614daa909ff744195bcc45464472.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that: \n\n- The hypotenuse has length $1$ since it is a radius of the unit circle.\n\n- The adjacent and opposite sides have lengths $x$ and $y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, using the definitions of $\\cos\\theta$ and $\\sin\\theta$, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{x}{1} = x, \\qquad  \\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{1} = y.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055960 #edb/ref 1065 2494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055960 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e7838544-050b-5270-a16a-1679e3489a02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055960 #edb/ref 1067 "A Proof of the Unit Circle Property" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055963 #edb/ref 1064 "To find the tangent of an angle using the unit circle, we need to use the relationship\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTo see why this is true, let's take a look at the following triangle.\n\n![](images/99/99b51d7d25a266dbfda7839be6bafc335b181ecd6dc74224fcb0557180f872aa.png)\n\nFor this particular triangle, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{z}, \\qquad \\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{x}{z}, \\qquad \\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nWhen we take $\\sin\\theta$ and divide it by $\\cos\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{y}{z}÷\\frac{x}{z} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{z}\\cdot \\frac{z}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\cdot z}{x\\cdot z} \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\cdot \\cancel{z}}{x\\cdot \\cancel{z}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough this proof is only for acute angles, the identity is true for any angle, provided that $\\tan\\theta$ is well-defined.\n\nFinally, since $\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan\\theta},$ it immediately follows that\n\n$$\n\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\\right)} = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055963 #edb/ref 1065 2725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055963 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a17ae159-de66-547d-8e9a-2190b512ab3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055963 #edb/ref 1067 "The Relationship Between Sine, Cosine, and Tangent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550345 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186551367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550345 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186551372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550345 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186551440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550345 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550345 #edb/ref 1045 "Properties of the Unit Circle in the First Quadrant" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550345 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186551372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550345 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550345 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550345 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "415fc07e-39c3-5797-979e-ef79737b49e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921865