17592186045417] [17592186551142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186551035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551142 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551142 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551142 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551142 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "82c3804a-ba42-5eda-8d70-9caba3ae75d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551155 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551155 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551155 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551155 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551155 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9194ee95-2b5a-5c6e-af69-3efb185447ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551197 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551197 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551197 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551197 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551197 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d1767cf0-549a-5130-876d-9403ed25774d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551250 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551250 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124392" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551250 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551250 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e5/e597e317f32a2a9a3757ce583a2b053f4cba1da1a2119c572671e7edc96c61e4.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{6}{7},\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551250 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "29930a9c-51d8-56d8-87b6-15535f5105d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551250 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/fb/fb0da765a857607ce0c8f13690efaa2f0d504ccaa51899a23044ee4ed17f0aa5.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{6}{7}$ and $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7})}{(\\frac{6}{7})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{6}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551269 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551269 #edb/ref 1069 "q-267389" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551269 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551269 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fe/fe2f1266db9f675a2c82803728d37f53d0995c25cf61f74304a731c3d21c64c8.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6},\\frac{5}{6})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ?$ Do not round your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551269 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44100483-c880-5325-8cb2-5b4178bd1ae8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551269 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/48/489a919487ffe7051a27f736ff8ca210e4c246bceac42055e31f5150bc4e094b.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{5}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551277 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551277 #edb/ref 1069 "q-267419" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551277 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551277 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a8/a8fc1b09d51bf0d7ac526a917b2341015b787b3098627bedd25e1638ecf041cc.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$ Do not round your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551277 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5237ac2b-249d-5de5-a0f0-312a03200ea8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551277 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/fd/fdddfbe8431756e0e0341ad6576383764eb88519cc0454280ce4d443559d3536.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}{(\\frac{2}{3})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551367 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186551440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551367 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1263712a-3a0a-57fc-89c3-d4db066a7847" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551367 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186551269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551372 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186551367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551372 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d9f8358-1b2c-5a6e-9744-40ad486a7663" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551372 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186551250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592